Anything which is a change in the price level is shown as a movement along the AD curve.

=== So why does it slope down from left to right?
+ When overall level of prices is relatively low, purchasing power of income is relatively
  high.
  So a low overall price means relatively high consumption.
+ When prices are low, #text(weight: "bold")[interest rates] also tend to be relatively
  low.
  This may encourage both investment and consumption (cost of borrowing affected)
+ When UK prices are relatively low compared to world prices, this increases the 
  competitiveness of UK goods.
  Therefore, #text(weight: "bold")[exports ↑ and imports ↓]

== Factors Affecting Aggregate Demand

=== What can cause a rightward shift in AD?
- A reduction in income tax, so disposable income rises, and therefore consumption 
  rises.
- A reduction in interest rates, so loans 'cost' relatively less, savings make less 
  money, and more money is spent.
- An increase in government spending
- An improved competitiveness performance

*NB: when interest rates decrease, loans cost less and savings make less money.*


=== What can cause a leftward shift in AD?
- An increase in income tax, so disposable income falls, and therefore consumption 
  falls.
- An increase in interest rates, so loans 'cost' more, savings make more money, and 
  less money is spent.
- A decrease in government spending.
- A decreased competitiveness performance.



