== Theories of Consumption

=== Keynes
- Keynes was the first economist to link current consumption with current disposable
  income. 
- He feared economic stagnation as households became wealthier and their MPC fell.
- His analysis was that poor households have a higher MPC than rich households. This
  justified redistribution of income from rich to poor households to boost
  consumption and stimulate growth. 
- *Short run MPC is viewed as stable.*

=== Life Cycle Hypothesis: Franco Modigliani and Albert Ando
Current consumption is not based upon current income alone. Rather, households form a
view about their likely/projected income *over their whole lifetime* and base current
spending decisions on that, with the aim of maintaining relatively consistent
material living standards.

=== Permanent Income Hypothesis: Milton Friedman
Milton Friedman defined _permanent income_ as the income a household could spend
without changing its wealth over time.
Households seek to gain this permanent income - they try to avoid diminishing their
wealth over time.

Therefore, redistribution of income is not justified, because *a household's short run
MPC is not stable.*

== Investment
Investment is the addition to the *capital stock* of the economy.

It is the purchase of capital goods which are then used to create other goods and
services. This differs from *saving*, which is the creation of financial obligations.

As time goes on, the value of the capital stock depreciates over time. This is called
*deprecation.*

_Gross Investment_ - measures investment before deprecation
_Net Investment_ - gross investment minus the value of deprecation

Investment can be made in:
- physical capital
- human capital

